Q: Open Browser with URL WPF A: <h3>Using Command Parameter</h3>
<p>You will notice below, that rather than binding to a property which holds the URL, we can pass in the URL directly via a command paramter.</p>
<p>The xaml as below:</p>
<pre><code>
&lt;TextBlock&gt;
     &lt;Hyperlink CommandParameter=&quot;https://www.tek1.com.au/precast/bubble-deck/how-does-tek1-do-bubble-deck-our-shop-drawing-processes#1-layout/&quot;
     Command=&quot;{Binding NavHomeViewCommand}&quot; &gt; Help
     &lt;/Hyperlink&gt;
&lt;/TextBlock&gt;
</code></pre>
<h3>MVVM model</h3>
<p>The view is bound to an MVVM instance.</p>
<p>In this case, RelayCommand is from a framework: <a href="https://github.com/lbugnion/mvvmlight/blob/master/GalaSoft.MvvmLight/GalaSoft.MvvmLight%20(PCL)/Command/RelayCommand.cs" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/lbugnion/mvvmlight/blob/master/GalaSoft.MvvmLight/GalaSoft.MvvmLight%20(PCL)/Command/RelayCommand.cs</a> But you may roll your own:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public RelayCommand&lt;string&gt; NavHomeViewCommand =&gt; new RelayCommand&lt;string&gt;((commandName) =&gt; NavigateToPage(commandName), (comandName) =&gt; true);        

public void NavigateToPage(string destinationurl)
{            
    var sInfo = new System.Diagnostics.ProcessStartInfo(destinationurl)
    {
        UseShellExecute = true,
    };
    System.Diagnostics.Process.Start(sInfo);
}
</code></pre>
<h3>The basic point:</h3>
<ul>
<li>You can use command param to pass in a URL itself, rather than saving it to the a property in a view model. I find the above documented approach much more intuitive.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do what works for you!</p>
